### Have any courts, journalists, or government bodies formally debunked the specific claim that the Clintons ran a child sex-trafficking ring out of a Washington, D.C., pizzeria?
Answer: Yes, multiple authorities have formally debunked the Pizzagate claim. The Washington, D.C. police describedPizzagate as a "fictitious online conspiracy theory" in their official statement following the 2016 incident, and the man who fired an assault rifle inside Comet Ping Pong to "self-investigate" the claims, Edgar Maddison Welch, pleaded guilty and was sentenced to four years in prison after acknowledging, "The intel on this wasn't 100 percent." Additionally, fact-checking journalists at Lead Stories have documented that the purported evidence—such as FBI documents supposedly proving pizza-related code words—does not actually support the allegations, noting that the real FBI bulletin "discusses symbols and logos used by pedophiles, but makes no mention of code words related to pizza," and Comet Ping Pong owner James Alefantis publicly denied the conspiracy claims in the wake of the attack.

Source URL: https://leadstories.com/hoax-alert/2020/09/fact-check-pizzagate-is-not-real.html

